  "text": "To solve the problem, let's break it down step by step:\n\n1. Since $PR = PQ$, triangle $PQR$ is isosceles with $PQR$ as the vertical angle.\n2. The interior angle bisector of $\\angle P$ will intersect at $S$.\n3. The circumcircle of $\\triangle PSR$ has a diameter of 14 cm. Therefore, its radius is $7$ cm, hence $\\widehat{PRS} = 140^\\circ$.\n4. To find the area of $\\triangle PQR$, we need the distance from the apex to the middle point of the base side, since it's a simple isosceles triangle.\n\nFirst, note that we need to find the distance $SO$, i.e., the distance from the origin to the middle point of the side $QR$.\n\nUsing the property of the triangle bisector, we know:\n$$\\frac{\\sin(A/2)}{\\sin(B/2)} = \\frac{|OQ|}{|OR|} = \\frac{|QR|}{2}$$\n\nPlugging in $\\widehat{PRS} = 140^\\circ$, we find:\n$$SO = \\frac{QR}{|2s|} \\cdot 2s \\sin(\\widehat{PRS}/2) \\cdot \\cos(\\widehat{PRS}/2) = 7$$\nwhere $\\sin(\\widehat{PRS}/2) = \\frac{2}{7} \\Rightarrow \\cos(\\widehat{PRS}/2) = \\frac{39}{49}$\n\nUse trigonometric identities $\\tan^2(\\alpha) = \\frac{\\sin^2(\\alpha)}{1-\\sin^2(\\alpha)}$ to get:\n\n$$\\tan\\left(\\frac{\\alpha}{2}\\right) = \\frac{\\sin(\\alpha)}{1+\\cos(\\alpha)}$$\n\nGiven:\n$$\\tan\\left(\\frac{140^\\circ}{2}\\right) = \\frac{\\frac{2}{7}}{1+\\frac{39}{49}} = \\frac{\\frac{14}{49}}{\\frac{88}{49}} = \\frac{14}{88} = \\frac{7}{44}$$\n\n$$\\frac{\\sqrt{140^\\circ + 180^\\circ}}{2} \\sin(70^\\circ) = \\approx \\frac{50^\\circ}{1} \\left(\\frac{2}{7}\\right) \\left(\\frac{1066}{494}\\right) \\approx 1.017$$\n\n$$\\frac{50^\\circ}{1} = \\sqrt{\\tan(70^\\circ/2)} \\approx \\sqrt{\\frac{7}{44}} \\approx 0.372$$\n\nCombining all, we obtain:\n\n$$RI = 50^\\circ \\cdot 0.372 \\approx 44.2 \\times 1 = 50$$\n\nHence, the final answer is:\n\n$$\\boxed{150\\text{cm}^2}$$",
